On Sunday, November 10, 2013 2:25:41 PM UTC-8, jerk-o wrote:
> When you read a scroll of identify how do you choose what to identify first?

1) Amulets
Amulets can be extremely useful, and many are hard to identify without formal
identification.  An "oLS can be a life saver.  An " of unchanging can be good
or bad depending on the circumstances.  An " versus poison is perhaps the most
useful if you ID it before you have poison resistance.

2) 300 zm scrolls
A scroll of genocide can be an escape item.  A scroll of charging can be very
useful.  A scroll of punishment can be really annoying.  

3) a /oW
to prevent the accidental premature wrest.  If you get a /oW with 5-7 charges, you should be able to win regardless of your knowledge of the rest of your 
equipment.  If you destroy the wand on your 2nd wish, your chances are much
lower.

4) 150 zm ring if I don't have poison resistance
If I don't have poison resistance, that's a pretty high priority.  Extrinsic
poison resistance provides an easy path to internal poison resistance.  Some of
the other 150 zm rings are useful as well, just not as important.

5) 200 zm ring 
Some useful rings that can be hard to otherwise identify.

6) Helms that aren't dropped by soldiers
You can't really try un-ID'd helms on out of fear of opposite alignment, and a helm of brilliance is great for a spellcaster.

7) Escape-item worthy wand
Wand of fire/lightning/teleport

8) Other stuff
At some point, you wind up with several blessed scrolls of ID, and prioritization is less important.  Also, specific needs of the game become
important.  E.g., If you've got a stack of extra healing that you'd like to be 
full healing, you might want to ID your 150 zm potions.  

=-=-=-=-=-=-=

I think it's quite reasonable to read uncursed scrolls of ID in the early game.
It might take you a while to get potions of holy water, and in the meantime, a
soldier ant can kill you while you have a ?oG and ?oTaming in your backpack.
By the time you can bless stuff, you're generally more survivable than you are
at the start.

=-=-=-=-=-=-=

I don't usually have hunger problems.
After Sokoban, I've never had a hunger problem.  
I never ID rings with sinks.  
I don't think slow digestion is an especially high priority, but it can be nice.

=-=-=-=-=-=-=

If I get a 300 zm ring, I make it a priority to ID it, but I usually ID
it by trying it on.  The mines usually have known teleport traps for testing TC
and non-lethal friendly monsters for testing conflict.

